The most important person in the kingdom?
At that time, the disciples came to Jesus. They asked him, "Who is the most important person in the kingdom of heaven?"
Jesus came and called a little child to come to him. He made the child stand among them.
Jesus said: "What I am about to tell you is true. You must change and become like little children. If you do not do this, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven."
Everyone who becomes humble like this child is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven.
"Whoever receives a little child like this in my name receives me."
But what if one of these young people who believe in me were to lead another to sin? If he does that, it would be better for him to have a large millstone tied around his neck and be drowned in the depths of the sea.
Oh, that it were a curse upon the world because of the things that lead people into sin! Things like that ought to come to pass. But oh, that it were a curse upon those who cause it!
If your hand or foot causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of heaven with one hand or foot, than to go to hell with both hands and feet. In hell, the fire burns forever.
If your eye causes you to sin, tear it out and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of heaven with one eye than to have two eyes and be thrown into hellfire. The story of the lost sheep.
Know that you do not despise even one of these little ones. This is what I say to you. Their angels in heaven can see the face of my Father in heaven."
<.>
What do you think? Suppose a man has 100 sheep, and one of them goes astray. Would he not leave the 99 sheep on the hills? Would he not go and search for the one that went astray?
What I am going to tell you is true. If that sheep is found, he is happier with this sheep than with 99 sheep that were not lost.
The same applies to your father in heaven. He does not want any of these little ones to perish. When someone wrongs you.
Your brother wronged you, so go to him. Tell him what he did wrong. Keep the matter between you two. If he listens to you, you have him back.
But if he will not listen to you, then take with you one or two more, that every matter may be established by the testimony of two or three witnesses." (Deuteronomy 19:15)
And if he refuses to listen to witnesses? Then report it to the church. And if he refuses to listen even to the church? Then do not treat him as a brother. Treat him as you would an unbeliever or a tax collector.
What I am about to tell you is real. What you bind on earth will be bound in heaven. What you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.
Again, here's what I'm telling you. Suppose the two of you on earth agree on anything you ask for. My Father in heaven will do it for you.
Where two or three are gathered together in my name, there am I among them." The servant who had no mercy.
Peter came to Jesus. He asked, "Lord, how many times must I forgive my brother when he sins against me? Up to seven times?"
Jesus answered, "I tell you, not seven times, but seventy-seven times."
The kingdom of heaven is like a king who wanted to collect all the money that was owed to him by his servants.
When the king started doing that, a man who owed him millions of dollars was brought in.
The man could not pay. So his master ordered him. The man, his wife, his children, and everything he possessed had to be sold to pay off his debt.
The servant fell to his knees before him. "Give me time," he pleaded. "I will return everything."
His masters felt pity for him. They forgave him his debt and allowed him to leave.
But then that waiter came out and found one of the other waiters, who owed him a few dollars. He grabbed him and began to strangle him. "Pay me what you owe me!" he said.
The other server fell to his knees. 'Give me time,' he pleaded. 'I will pay you.'
But the first servant refused. Instead, he went and had the man thrown into prison. The man would be held there until he could repay his debt.
The other servants saw what happened. This worried them a great deal. They went and told their master everything that had happened.
Then the Lord called the first servant. "You wicked servant," he said. "I forgave you all that debt because you begged me.
Why did you not show mercy to the other servant as he showed mercy to you?
In a rage, his master handed him over to the guards. He will be punished until he pays back everything he owes.
In this way, my father in heaven will deal with each one of you, unless you forgive your brother from your heart.
Jesus teaches about divorce.
When Jesus had finished saying these things, he left Galilee and went into the region of Judea on the other side of the Jordan River.
Large crowds were accompanying him. He healed them there.
Some Pharisees came to test him. They asked him, "Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for just any reason?"
Jesus answered, "Haven't you read that at the beginning the Creator 'made them male and female'? (Genesis 1:27)
He said, "Therefore a man shall leave his father and his mother, and shall cleave to his wife; and they shall become one flesh." (Genesis 2:24)
They have become one flesh; therefore, what God has joined together, let no one separate."
They said, "What is the reason that Moses commanded that a man can give his wife a bill of divorce and send her away?"
Jesus replied, "Moses allowed you to divorce your wives because you were being stubborn. But it was not like that from the beginning."
Thus I tell you. Whoever divorces his wife and marries another woman commits adultery. A man has no right to divorce his wife unless she is unfaithful to him."
The disciples said to him, "If such is the case between husband and wife, it is better not to marry."
Jesus replied, "Not everyone can accept the idea of remaining single. Only those who have been helped to live without marriage can accept it."
Some men are unable to have children because they were born that way. Some may have been born that way by other people. Others may have made themselves that way in order to serve the kingdom of heaven. Whoever can accept living in this way must do so." The little children are brought to Jesus.
Some people brought their little children to Jesus. They wanted him to place his hands on the children and pray for them. But the disciples told the people to stop.
Jesus said, "Let the little children come to me, and do not hinder them. For the kingdom of heaven belongs to such as these."
Jesus laid his hands on them. Then he went from there. Jesus and the Rich Young Man
A man came to Jesus. He asked, "Teacher, what good thing must I do to have eternal life?"
"Why do you ask me about what is good?" Jesus answered. "There is only one who is good. If you want to enter into the kingdom, keep the commandments."
"Who?" the man asked. Jesus said, " 'Do not murder. Do not commit adultery. Do not steal. Do not give false testimony.
And honor your father and your mother. (Exodus 20:12-16; Deuteronomy 5:16-20) And you shall love your neighbor as yourself. " (Leviticus 19:18)
The young man said, "I have obeyed all those commandments." "What do I still need to do?"
Jesus said, "If you want to be perfect, go, sell your possessions and give to the poor. You will have treasure in heaven. Then come, follow me."
When the young man heard this, he went away sad. He was very rich.
Jesus said to his disciples, "Truly I tell you, it is difficult for those who have wealth to enter the kingdom of heaven."
Again I tell you, it is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for someone who is rich to enter the kingdom of God."
When the disciples heard this, they were truly amazed. They asked, "Who then can be saved?"
Jesus looked at them and said, "For man, this is impossible. But with God, all things are possible."
Peter answered him, "We have left everything to follow you! What will be given to us as a reward?"
Jesus said to them, "What I tell you is true. When everything is reformed, the Son of Man will sit on his glorious throne. Then you who have followed me will also sit on twelve thrones. You will judge the twelve tribes of Israel.
Everyone who has left houses or families or fields on my account will receive 100 times as much. They will also receive eternal life.
But many who are first will be last, and many who are last will be first.
The story of the workers in the vineyard.
The kingdom of heaven is like a man of noble birth who went to a distant country to acquire for himself authority, so that he might return and secure his kingdom. But when he returned, the kingdom had been seized by another king, who did not want to yield authority to the first king. So the first king went out and engaged in battle, and in the course of the battle his army was routed and he himself was killed.
He agreed to pay them the usual wage for a day's work. Then he sent them into his vineyard.
At about nine o'clock in the morning he went out again. He saw others standing in the marketplace doing nothing.
He said to them, "You also go and work in my vineyard, and I will give you what is right."
They left. "He went out again around noon and at three o'clock and did the same thing."
Around five o'clock he went out and found others standing around him. He asked them, 'Why have you been standing here all day and doing nothing?'
Because he did not hire us, they answered. "He said to them, 'You also go and work in my vineyard.'"
When evening came, the owner of the vineyard spoke to the one who was in charge of the workers. He said, "Call the workers and give them their pay. Start with the others who were hired last. Then go on to the first ones."
The employees who were hired around five o'clock came. Each received the usual daily wage.
When those first hired came, they expected to receive more. But each of them also received his day's wage.
When they received it, they began to suspect the owner.
They said, "These people you hired the last time only worked for one hour." "You paid them the same amount you paid us. We did most of the work and were under the hot sun all day."
Companions, one of them answered. "My friend," he said, "I am being fair with you. Did you not agree to work for the usual day's wage?"
Take your money and go. I want to give the same wage that I gave you to those I hired before.
Do I not have the right to do what I want with my own money? Do you feel cheated because I gave generously to others?
Be silent, for whoever has, will be given more, and whoever does not have, even what they have will be taken away from them. The first will be last, and the last will be first.
Jesus was going up to Jerusalem. On the way, he took the twelve disciples aside to talk to them.
He said, "We are going to Jerusalem." "The Son of Man will be delivered over to the chief priests and the teachers of the law. They will condemn him to death."
Then they will hand him over to people who are not Jewish. People will mock him and whip him. They will nail him to a cross. On the third day, he will rise from the dead!" A mother asks Jesus to help her
The mother of the sons of Zebedee came to Jesus with her sons. Sitting at his feet, she asked him for a request.
"What do you want?" Jesus asked. "Promise me that one of my two sons may sit at your right in your kingdom," she said. "And promise that the other may sit at your left."
Jesus said to them, "You do not know what you are asking. Can you drink the cup that I am to drink?" They said to him, "We can."
Jesus said to them, "You will surely drink from my cup. But it is not for me to say who will sit at my right hand or at my left. These places belong to those for whom my Father has prepared them."
The two pupils listened on Sunday the eleventh, the other two to this. They were angry at the two brothers.
Jesus came and called them together. He said, "You know about the rulers of the Gentiles; for they exercise authority over their people, and they make their officers act in an authoritative manner."
You are not. Instead, everyone among you who wants to be important must be your servant.
And everyone who wants to be first must be your slave.
Be like the Son of Man. He did not come to be served, but to serve others. He came to give his life as a ransom for many people. Two blind men receive their sight.
Jesus and his disciples leave Jericho. A large crowd follows him.
There were two blind men sitting by the roadside. When they heard that Jesus was passing by, they cried out, "Lord, Son of David, have mercy on us!"
The crowd told them to stop and to calm down, but the two men shouted even louder, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on us!"
When Jesus stopped, he called out to them. "What do you want me to do for you?" he asked.
"Oh Lord," they answered, "we want to be able to see."
Jesus felt deep concern for them. He touched their eyes. Immediately they could see. And they followed him.
Jesus enters Jerusalem.
As they approached Jerusalem, they came to Bethphage, which was on the Mount of Olives. Jesus sent two disciples ahead.
He told them, "Go to the village ahead of you. As soon as you reach it, you will find a donkey tied up. Its mare will be with it. Untie them both and bring them back to me."
If someone tells you something, tell them God needs them. The owner will make them leave immediately."
This has happened so that what was spoken through the prophet might be fulfilled. He says,
"Say to the daughter of Zion, 'Behold, your king is coming to you, gentle and riding on a donkey, on a colt, the foal of a beast of burden.'" (Zechariah 9:9)
The disciples went and did what Jesus had asked them to do.
Bring the donkey and the horse. Put their clothes on them. Then Jesus sat on the clothes.
Many people spread their clothes on the road. Others cut branches from trees and spread them on the road.
Some people went ahead of him, and others followed him. They all cried out, "Hosanna to the Son of David!" "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!" (Psalm 118:26) "Hosanna in the highest!"
When Jesus entered Jerusalem, the entire city was in an uproar. The people asked, "Who is this?"
The crowd answered, "This is Jesus. He is the Prophet from Nazareth in Galilee." Jesus Cleanses the Temple
Jesus entered the temple area. He began driving out those who were buying and selling there. He overturned the tables of those who were exchanging money. He also overturned the seats of those who were selling doves.
He said to them, "It is written, 'The Lord has said, "My house shall be called a house of prayer"'" (Isaiah 56:7). "But you are making it 'a den of robbers.'" (Jeremiah 7:11)
Blind people and those who were crippled came to Jesus at the temple. There he healed them.
The chief priests and the teachers of the law saw the wonderful things he did. They saw the children in the temple area shouting, "Hosanna to the Son of David!" But when they saw all this, they were indignant.
They said to Him, "Do you hear what these children are saying?" "Yes," Jesus answered. "Have you not read in the Scriptures? It is written, 'The little ones will become a joyful community for me.' " (Psalm 8: 2)
Then Jesus left the people and went out of the city to Bethany. He spent the night there. The fig tree withers
Early in the morning, Jesus was on his way back to Jerusalem. He was hungry.
He saw a fig tree by the roadside. He went to it, but found nothing on it but leaves. Then he said to it, "May you never again bear fruit!" Suddenly the tree dried up.
And when the disciples saw this, they were amazed. "How did the fig tree wither so quickly?" they asked.
Jesus answered, "What I tell you is truth. You must have faith and not doubt. Then you can do the same as what they did to the fig tree. And you can say to a mountain, 'Go and throw yourself into the sea.' And it will be done."
If you believe, you will receive what you ask for when you pray." Jesus' authority is being questioned.
Jesus entered the temple courts. As he taught there, the chief priests and the elders of the people came to him. "By what authority are you doing these things?" they asked. "Who gave you this authority?"
Jesus replied, "I will ask you one question. If you answer me, I will tell you by what authority I do these things."
From where did John's baptism come? From heaven or from men?" They discussed it with each other. They said, "If we say, 'From heaven,' he will ask, 'Why then did you not believe him?'"
But what if we say "of the men"? We fear people. Everyone thinks John is a prophet."
They answered Jesus, "We do not know." And Jesus said, "Neither will I tell you by what authority I do these things. The Parable of the Two Sons.
What do you think about this? A man had two sons. He went to the first and said, 'My son, go and work today in the vineyard.'
"I will not," the son answered. But he later changed his mind and went.
Then the father went to the other son. He said the same thing. The son answered, 'I will, sir.' But he did not go.
Which of the two sons did what his father wanted?" "The first," they answered. Jesus said to them, "The things I say to you are true. Tax collectors and prostitutes will enter the kingdom of God ahead of you.
John came to show you the right way to live. And you did not believe him. But tax collectors and prostitutes believed him. I saw this. But even then you did not leave your sins and believe him. The story of the tenants.
Listen to another story. A man owned a piece of land, he planted a vineyard. He put a fence around it. He dug a pit to press the wine in. He also built a watchtower. He rented the vineyard to some farmers. Then he went on a trip.
When the harvest was near, he sent his servants to the tenants. He told the servants to collect his share of the fruit.
But the tenants seized his slaves. They beat one of them. They killed another. They threw stones at a third to kill him.
Then the man sent other servants to the tenants. He sent more than he had sent at first. The tenants treated them in the same way.
Finally, he sent his son to them. He said, "They will respect my son."
But the tenants saw the son coming. They said to each other, "This is the one who will get all the owner's property someday. Let's kill him. Then everything will be ours."
So they seized him and cast him outside the vineyard. Then they killed him.
When the vineyard owner returns, what will he do with those tenants?
They said, "He will destroy those wicked tenants," "and he will rent the vineyard to other tenants. They will give him his share of the harvest at the time of the harvest."
Jesus said to them: "Have you not read what the Scriptures say: 'The stone that the builders rejected has become the cornerstone. This was the Lord's doing, and it is marvelous in our eyes'? (Psalm 118: 22, 23)"
This is what I say to you. The kingdom of God will be taken from you. It will be given to people who will produce its fruit.
Everyone who falls on that stone will be shattered into pieces. But the stone itself will crush anyone who falls on it."
The chief priests and the Pharisees listened to the stories about Jesus. They knew that he was speaking about them.
So they looked for a way to arrest him. But they were afraid of the crowd. For the people believed that Jesus was a prophet.
The Wedding Dinner Story
Jesus told them more stories. He said:
Thus is the kingdom of heaven. A king prepared a wedding feast for his son.
He sent his slaves to those who had been invited to the dinner. The slaves told them to come. But they refused.
Then he sent some other servants. "Tell those who have been invited that I have prepared my dinner. I have killed my fattened ox and my livestock. Everything is ready. Come to the wedding feast."
But the people paid no attention. One went to his field. Another went to his work.
The rest seized his slaves. They treated them harshly and then killed them.
The king became very angry. He sent his army to destroy them. Those murderers were killed, and their city was burned.
Then the king said to his servants, "The wedding feast is ready. But those I invited were not worthy to attend."
Go to the street corners. Invite to dinner anyone you can find.
The servants went out into the streets. They gathered all the people they could find, whether they were good or bad. Soon the wedding hall was filled with guests.
The king entered to see the guests. He noticed a man there who was not wearing wedding clothes.
My friend, how did you get in here without your wedding clothes? he asked him. The man couldn't think of anything to say.
Then the king said to his servants, "Tie his hands and feet. Throw him outside into the darkness. There people will weep and grind their teeth."
Many are called, but few are chosen." Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar?
The Pharisees went out. They plotted to entrap Jesus with his own words.
They sent their followers to him. They sent the Herodians with them. "Teacher," they said, "we know that you are a man of integrity. You teach the way of God in truth. You do not allow others to tell you what to do or say. It does not matter how important they are."
They said to us, "What do you think? Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar, or not?"
But Jesus, knowing their evil scheming, said, "You pretenders! Why are you trying to trap me?"
"Show me the coin that people use to pay the tax." They brought him a silver coin.
He asked them: "Who is in this picture? And who are these words?"
Caesarea, respond. Then he said to them, "Give to Caesar the things that are Caesar's. And give to God the things that are God's."
And when they heard that, they were astonished. So they left him and went away. Marriage when the dead rise.
On the same day, the Sadducees came to Jesus with a question. They do not believe that people are raised from the dead.
They said, "Teacher, this is what Moses told us. 'If a man dies without children, his brother must marry the widow. He must have children to carry on his brother's name.'
There were seven brothers among us. The first one got married and died. Since he had no children, he left his wife for his brother.
The same thing was happening to the second and third brothers. It continued until the seventh brother.
Finally, the woman died.
Now, when the dead rise, who is his wife? Each of the seven was married to her."
Jesus replied, "You are wrong, because you do not know the Scriptures. And you do not know the power of God."
When the dead rise, they will not marry. Nor will their parents give them away in marriage. They will be like the angels in heaven.
What about the dead that are resurrected? Have you not read what God has revealed to you?
He said, "I am the God of Abraham. I am the God of Isaac. And I am the God of Jacob." (Exodus 3:6) He is not the God of the dead. He is the God of the living."
When the crowd heard this, they were amazed at his teaching. The most important commandment
When the Pharisees heard that the Sadducees had not been able to respond to Jesus, they assembled.
One of them, an expert in the law, tried to test Jesus with a question.
"Teacher," he asked, "which is the most important commandment in the law?"
Jesus answered, " 'Love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your mind.' " (Deuteronomy 6:5)
This is the first and most important commandment.
And the second is like it: 'Love your neighbor as yourself.' (Leviticus 19:18)
All that is written in the Law and the Prophets is based on these two commandments." Son of whom is Christ?
The Pharisees met. Jesus asked them:
"What do you think about the Messiah? Whose son is he?" "The son of David," they answered.
He said to them, "Then why is David called 'Lord'? The Holy Spirit spoke through David himself. David said:
The Lord said to my Lord, "Sit at my right hand until I make your enemies your footstool." (Psalm 110:1)
"If David calls him 'Lord,' how can he be David's son?"
No one could answer him with a single word. And from that day on, no one dared to ask him any questions.
Jesus condemns the Pharisees and teachers of the law.
Jesus spoke to the crowds and to his disciples.
He said, "The scholars of the law and the Pharisees sit on the seat of Moses."
You must obey them. Do everything they tell you to do. But do not do what they do. For they do not practice what they preach.
They tie heavy loads and throw them onto the shoulders of others. But they themselves do not wish to move them, not even with a single finger.
All that they do is done for others to see. On their foreheads and arms they wear small boxes containing texts from the Bible. They make the boxes very wide. And they make the strings on their coats very long.
They like to sit in the place of honor at banquets. They also like to get the most important seats in synagogues.
They like to be greeted in the markets. They like it when people call them "my Lord."
But he should not be called "my Lord." You have one master, and you are all brothers.
Do not call anyone on earth "father". For you have one Father, and He is in heaven.
You are not in need of being called "Teacher." You have one Teacher, and that is Christ.
The most important person among you will be your servant.
Whoever exalts himself will be humbled. And whoever is humbled will be exalted.
Woe to you, teachers of the law and you, Pharisees, you hypocrites! You travel over land and sea to convert one proselyte, and when he becomes a Jew, he is no better off than before. Woe to you, teachers of the law and you, Pharisees, you hypocrites! You build tombs for the prophets and decorate the tombs of the righteous. And you say, 'If we had lived in the days of our ancestors, we would not have participated with them in shedding the blood of the prophets.' You snakes, you offspring of vipers! How will you escape being condemned to hell? Therefore, I tell you, every careless word that people speak, they will give an account for it on the Day of Judgment. For by your words you will be acquitted, and by your words you will be condemned.'
<.>
Oh, if only you, you teachers and Pharisees! You are the tradition-observers! You travel everywhere to make one proselyte, and when he becomes a proselyte, he becomes twice as much a son of hell as you are.
O blind guides, who are blind in heart! You say, 'If anyone swears by the temple, it means nothing. But if anyone swears by the gold of the temple, he must keep his oath.'
You are blind and ignorant! Which is more important? Is it the gold? Or is it the temple which makes the gold sacred?
It also says, 'If anyone swears by the altar, it is nothing; but if anyone swears by the gift that is on it, he is bound to keep his oath.'"
O ye blind men! Which of these is the more important? Is it the gift? Or is it the altar upon which the gift is made sacred?
Whoever swears by the altar, swears by it and by everything on it.
And whoever swears an oath by the name of the temple swears an oath by its name and by the one who dwells in it.
And everyone who swears an oath in the name of heaven swears an oath in the name of God's throne and of the one who sits upon it.
"Oh, you hypocrites, blind guides, self-indulgent monuments to your own ego! You are the ones who follow the traditions of men, rather than the commandments of God! You pay tithes on your spices - mint, dill, cumin - but you neglect the weightier matters of the law: justice, mercy, and faithfulness! You should have practiced the latter without neglecting the former!"
I blind you! You take out the smallest insect from your food. But you swallow a whole camel!
Oh, if only you, teachers of the law and Pharisees! You are the ones who follow the traditions! You clean the outside of the cup and the dish. But the inside of you is full of greed. You only want to please yourselves.
Blind beggar! Clean first the inside of the cup and of the dish; then the outside will be clean also.
O you, the teachers of the law and the Pharisees! You are the ones who are pretending! You are like tombs that are painted white. They look beautiful on the outside. But on the inside they are full of the bones of the dead. They are also full of other things that are not pure and clean.
The same is true for you. From the outside, it looks like you are doing what is right. But on the inside, you are full of what is wrong. You pretend to be what you are not.
"Oh ye scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! Ye build the tombs of the prophets, and ye garnish the sepulchres of the righteous,
And you say, "If we had lived in the days of those who lived before us, we would not have done what they did. We would not have helped in the killing of the prophets."
You testify against yourselves. You acknowledge that you are the sons of those who killed the prophets.
Avoid the sins that were first committed by those who lived before you!
You are a den of poisonous snakes! How will you escape judgment by going to hell?
I will send you prophets and wise men and teachers. And some of them you will kill. And some of them you will crucify. And some of them you will torture in your temples. And you will drive them out of city to city.
You will pay the price for the blood of all righteous people who have been shed on earth. I mean from the blood of righteous Abel to the blood of Zechariah the son of Berekiah. Zechariah is the one you killed between the temple and the altar.
What I will tell you is real. All of this will happen to those who are living now.
Jerusalem! Jerusalem! You are killing the prophets and stoning the stones to kill those who have been sent to you. How often I have wanted to gather your people together. I wanted to be like a hen that gathers her chicks under her wings. But you would not let me!
Look, your house is still empty.
I tell you, you will not see me again until you say, "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord." (Psalm 118:26)
End Signs
Jesus went out of the temple. He was walking away when the disciples approached him. They wanted to call his attention to the temple buildings.
Jesus said, "Do you see all these things? Truly I tell you, not one stone will be left here upon another; every stone will be thrown down."
Jesus was sitting on the Mount of Olives. There, the disciples came to him privately. "Tell us," they said, "when will this happen? And what will be the sign of your coming? What will be the sign of the end?"
James answered, "Be careful! Be careful that no one deceive you."
Many will come in my name. They will say, "I am the Christ!" They will deceive many people.
You will hear about wars. And you will also hear people talking about future wars. Do not be afraid. These things must be. But the end has not come yet.
The state will fight the state. The kingdom will fight the kingdom. The people will go hungry. There will be earthquakes in many places.
All these are the beginning of the birth pangs.
Then people will hand you over to be treated harshly and killed. And all nations will hate you because of me.
At that time, many will depart from their faith. They will depart from one another. They will hand one another over to their enemies.
Many false prophets will appear. They will deceive many people.
Because evil is increasing, the love of most people will grow cold.
But the one who stands firm to the end will be saved.
This good news for the kingdom will be preached in the whole world. It will be a testimony to all nations. Then the end will come.
The prophet Daniel spoke about 'the abomination that makes desolate' (Daniel 9:27; 11:31; 12:11) One day you will see standing in the holy place. Let the reader understand this.
Then those who are in Judea must flee to the mountains.
No one is going up on the roof to go home and get anything.
Nobody in the field should have to go back for their coat.
It wouldn't be so bad in those days for pregnant women! Bad for breastfeeding mothers!
Pray lest you be forced to flee in winter or on the Sabbath.
There will be terrible suffering in those days. It will be worse than anything else that has happened in the world from the beginning of time until now. And there will never be anything like it again.
If the hour had not been shortened, no one would have been saved. But for the sake of God's chosen people, the hour will be shortened.
At that time, someone might tell you, "Look! Here is the Christ!" or, "There he is!" Do not believe it.
Christians and false prophets will appear. They will perform great miracles. They will attempt to deceive God's chosen ones if possible.
Look, I told you before.
If someone tells you "it is far in the desert," do not go there. Or if someone tells you "it is deep in the house," do not believe it.
The lightning that comes from the east can be seen in the west. It will be the same when the Son of Man comes.
Vultures will gather wherever there is a carcass.
After the terrible ordeal of those days, "there shall be a darkening of the sun, and the moon shall not give its light. And the stars shall be falling from heaven, and the powers of the heavens shall be shaken." (Isaiah 13:10; 34:4)
Then the sign of the Son of Man will appear in heaven, and all the nations of the earth will mourn. They will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven, with power and great glory.
He will send his angels with a loud trumpet call. He will gather his chosen people from all four directions. He will bring them from one end of the sky to the other.
Learn a lesson from the fig tree. As soon as its branches become tender and sprout leaves, you know that summer is near.
In the same way, when you see all those things happening, you know the end is near. It's right at the door.
What I am going to tell you is true. The people who are living at that time will certainly not die until all those things happen.
The heaven and the earth shall pass away: but my words shall not pass away. No man knows the day or the hour.
No one knows about that day or hour. Even the angels in heaven do not know. The Son does not know. Only the Father knows.
Remember how it was in the days of Noah. It will be the same when the Son of Man comes.
In the days before the flood, people were eating and drinking. They were marrying. They were giving their daughters in marriage. They were doing all those things until the day Noah entered the ark.
They had no knowledge of what would happen until the flood came and swept them all away. This is how it will be when the Son of Man comes.
Two men will be in the field. One will be taken, and the other will be left.
Two women will grind flour using a hand mill. One will be taken and the other will be left.
Therefore be ready, for you do not know on what day your Lord is coming.
You have to understand something. Suppose the homeowner knew at what time of night the thief would come. Then he would stay on the alert. He would not allow his house to be broken into.
So you also must be ready. Just as a thief comes, so the Son of Man will come at an hour that no one expects.
Assume a master puts one of his slaves in charge of the other servants in his house. The slave's task is to give them their food at the proper time. The master wants a faithful and wise slave for this.
It would be good for the slave if his master found him doing his work when the master returned.
What I am going to tell you is true. The master will put that servant in charge of all his possessions.
But suppose that this slave is wicked; suppose that he says to himself, "My master is going to be away a long time."
Suppose he begins to strike the other servants. And suppose he eats and drinks with those who drink heavily.
The master of that slave will return on a day the slave does not expect. He will return in an hour the slave does not know.
Then the master will cut him to pieces. He will send him to the place where the protesters go. There they will cry and gnash their teeth.
The Story of Ten Maidens Accompanying
Thus shall be the kingdom of heaven at that time. And she took ten virgins with lamps and went out to meet the bridegroom.
Five of them were stupid. Five were wise.
The ignorant ones took their lamps but they did not take olive oil with them.
Those who were wise took oil in their vessels with their lamps.
The groom did not come for a long time. Therefore, all the married brides became tired and fell asleep.
At midnight someone shouted, "Here is the bridegroom! Come to meet him!"
Then all the little brides woke up and put their lamps in readiness.
The ignorant said to the wise, "Give us some of your oil. Our lamps are going out."
"No," they replied. "It may not be enough for everyone. Instead, go to the one who is selling the oil. Buy some for yourselves."
So they went to buy oil. But while they were walking, the bridegroom arrived. So she went in with him, along with the bridesmaids who were ready for the wedding feast. Then she closed the door.
After that, the bride came to the other bride. "Sir! Sir!" she said. "Open the door for us!"
But he answered, "What I am going to tell you is true. I do not know you."
Keep it secret. You do not know either the day or the hour when the bridegroom will come. A story of three servants.
Again, this is what the kingdom of heaven will be like. A man was going on a journey. He sent his servants to his tenants and ordered them to prepare his harvest for him.
The man gave 10,000 dollars to one. He gave 4,000 dollars to another. And he gave 2,000 dollars to the third. The man gave each servant the amount he knew the servant could handle. Then he went on his journey.
The server that received $10,000 went directly and started to employ his money. He earned another $10,000.
The one who had 4000 dollars won 4000 more dollars.
But the man who received the 2000 dollars went and dug a hole in the ground. He hid his master's money there.
After a long time, the master of those slaves returned. He wanted to collect all the money they had earned.
The man who received 10,000 dollars brought the other 10,000 dollars. "Sir," he said, "you trusted me with 10,000 dollars. Look, I won another 10,000 dollars."
The Lord of the servant came and said to him, "You have done well, my good servant! You have been faithful in a few things. I will put you in charge of many things. Come and share your master's happiness!"
The man who had $4000 also came. "Sir," he said, "you trusted me with $4000. Look, I have won another $4000."
His master's reply was: "You have done well, good and faithful servant! You were faithful over a little; I will set you over many things. Come and share your master's happiness!"
Then came the man who had received the 2000 dollars. "Sir," he said, "I knew you were a hard man. You sow where you have not reaped. You gather where you have not scattered seed."
So I was afraid. I went out and put 2000 dollars underground. Look, this is what's yours."
His master answered him, "You wicked, lazy servant! Did you know that I harvest where I did not plant, and gather where I did not scatter?"
Well, you should have put my money in the bank. When I came back, I would have received it again with interest.
Then his master ordered the other servants, "Take 2000 dollars from him. Give it to the one who has 20000 dollars."
Everyone who has will get more. He will have more than enough. And what about anyone who does not have? What he has will be taken away from him.
Cast out that good-for-nothing slave. Out there in the darkness, people will weep and grind their teeth." Sheep and pigs.
The Son of Man will come in his glory. And all the angels will come with him. Then he will sit on his throne in the glory of heaven.
All nations will be gathered before him. He will divide the people into two groups. It will be like a shepherd who separates the sheep from the goats.
He will place the sheep on his right and the goats on his left.
Then the king will speak to those on his right. He will say, "My Father has blessed you. Come and receive what is yours. It is the kingdom that was prepared for you from the creation of the world."
I was hungry. And you gave me something to eat. I was thirsty. And you gave me something to drink. I was a stranger. And you invited me into your home.
I needed clothes. And you gave them to me. I was sick. And you took care of me. I was in prison. And you came to visit me.
Then the righteous who had done what was right would answer him. "Lord," they would ask, "when did we see you hungry and feed you? When did we see you thirsty and give you something to drink?"
When did we see you as a stranger and invite you into our homes? And when did we see you in need of clothes and give them to you?
When have we ever seen you sick or in prison and we went to visit you?
The King will answer, "What I tell you is true. Whatever I did to one of these least brothers of mine, I did to myself."
Then He will say to those on His left, 'Depart from Me, all you who are under God's curse, into the eternal fire, which has been prepared for the devil and his angels.'
I was hungry. But you did not give me anything to eat. I was thirsty. But you did not give me anything to drink.
I was a stranger. But you did not invite me into your home. I needed clothes. But you did not give me any. I was sick and a prisoner. But you did not take care of me.
They will also answer, 'Lord, when did we see you hungry or thirsty and did not help you? When did we see you as a stranger or in need of clothes or sick or in prison and did not help you?'
"He will reply, 'What I am about to tell you is true. Anything you did not do for one of these least brothers of mine, you did not do for me.'"
Then they go away to be punished for ever. But those who did what is right will get everlasting life.
The plan to kill Jesus.
Jesus said all these things, and then said to his disciples:
As you know, Easter is in two days. The Son of Man will be delivered to be crucified on the cross.
Then the chief priests and the elders of the people assembled. They met in the palace of Caiaphas, the high priest.
They made plans to arrest Jesus cunningly. They wanted to kill him.
"Not during the feast," they said. "Otherwise, people may cause an uproar." A woman poured perfume on Jesus.
Jesus was in Bethany. He was in the house of a man named Simon, who had a skin disease.
A woman came to Jesus with a special sealed jar of very expensive perfume. She poured the perfume on his head while he was at the table.
When the students saw this, they were angry. "Why is there such waste?" they asked.
The perfume could have been sold for a high price. The money could have been given to poor people.
Jesus perceived this. And he said to them: "Why do you trouble this woman? For she has done a beautiful thing to me.
You will not always have the poor with you. But you will not always have me.
She poured perfume on my body to prepare me for burial.
What I am about to tell you is true. What you have done will be proclaimed in any place where this good news is preached throughout the world. It will be proclaimed on her behalf." Judas agrees to hand Jesus over."
One of the twelve went to the chief priests. His name was Judas Iscariot.
They asked him, "What will you give me if I hand Jesus over to you?" And they counted out for him 30 pieces of silver.
Since then, Judas had been watching for an opportune time to hand Jesus over to them. The Lord's Supper
It was the first day of the Feast of Unleavened Bread. The disciples came to Jesus. They asked Him, "Where do You want us to prepare for You to eat the Passover?"
Go to the man in the city. Tell him, "The Lord says, 'My time is near. I will keep the Passover at your house with my disciples.'"
So the disciples did what Jesus had told them to do. They prepared the Passover meal.
When evening came, Jesus was reclining at the table with the twelve apostles.
While they were eating, he said, "What I am about to tell you is true. One of you will betray me to my enemies."
The disciples were deeply saddened. They began to take turns saying to him, "Is it I, Lord?"
Jesus answered, "The one who dips his hand in the bowl with me is the one who will betray me.
The Son of Man will go, just as it is written about him. But woe to that man by whom the Son of Man is betrayed! It would have been better for him if he had never been born."
Judas said, "I am the one who will betray him?" He said, "Is it not I, Lord?" Jesus answered, "Yes. It is you."
And as they were eating, Jesus took bread. And when He had given thanks, He broke it and gave it to them, saying, "Take, eat; this is My body."
Then he took the cup. He thanked and handed it over to them. He said, "All of you drink from it."
This is my blood of the new covenant. It is poured out to forgive the sins of many.
Thus he told them, "From now on I tell you, I will never drink of the fruit of the vine with you until the day when I drink it with you new in the kingdom of my Father."
Then they sang a hymn and went out to the Mount of Olives. Jesus says that Peter will fail.
Jesus said to them, "This night, all of you will abandon me, for it is written, 'I will strike the shepherd, and the sheep of the flock will be scattered' (Zechariah 13:7)."
But after I am raised from the dead, I will go ahead of you into Galilee."
Peter replied, "Others may stay away because of you. But I will never do that."
"What I am telling you is true," Jesus answered. "This will all happen tonight. Before the rooster crows, you will deny me three times."
But Peter said, "I must die with you. I will never say that I don't know you." And all the other disciples said the same thing. Jesus Prays in Gethsemane
Then Jesus went with his disciples to a place called Gethsemani. He said to them, "Sit here while I go over there and pray."
Peter took John and James, the sons of Zebedee, along with him. He began to feel sorrow and deep distress.
Then he said to them, "My soul is very sorrowful. I feel that I am about to die. Stay here. Watch with me."
He went a little further than that. Then he fell on his face to the ground. He prayed, saying, "My Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me. Nevertheless, not my will, but yours, be done."
Then he came back to his disciples and found them sleeping. "Could you men not watch with me for one hour?" Peter asked.
Look and pray. After Mac you will not make a mistake when you are in temptation. The spirit wants. But the flesh is weak.
Jesus went out again. He prayed, saying, "My Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me; yet not as I will, but as you will."
Then he went back. Once again he found them sleeping. They couldn't keep their eyes open.
So he left them and departed again. For the third time he prayed the same thing.
Then he came back to the disciples. He said to them, "Are you still sleeping and taking rest? Look! The hour is near. The Son of Man is about to be delivered to the sinners."
"Arise! Let us go! Behold, he who delivered me up to them!" The arrest of Jesus.
And while Jesus was speaking, Judas came. He was one of the twelve. And a great crowd with swords and clubs was with him. They had been sent by the chief priests and the elders of the people.
Judas, who intended to betray Jesus, arranged a signal with them. "The one I kiss is the man," he said. "Seize him."
Judas immediately went to Jesus. He said, "Greetings, Rabbi!" and kissed him.
Jesus answered, "My friend, do as you have come to do." Then the men stepped forward. They seized Jesus and arrested him.
At that moment, one of Jesus' disciples reached for his sword. He drew it and struck the high priest's servant. He cut off the servant's ear.
Jesus said to him, "Put your sword back in its place, for all who take the sword will perish by the sword."
Do you think I can't ask my father for help? He will send an army of more than 70,000 angels immediately.
But how will the Bible be fulfilled? They say it must happen this way.
At that time, Jesus spoke to the crowd. "Am I leading a band of armed men against you?" he asked. "Have you come out with swords and sticks to capture me? Every day I was sitting in the temple courts teaching. And you did not arrest me."
But all this was to fulfill the sayings of the prophets." Then all the multitude forsook him and fled. And Jesus was led away to the council.
Those who arrested Jesus took him to Caiaphas the high priest. There they met with the teachers of the law and the elders.
He was not far away, and Peter followed Jesus. He went straight to the courtyard of the high priest. He entered and sat with the guards to see what would happen.
The high priests and the entire Sanhedrin were searching for something they could use against Jesus. They wanted to kill him.
But they found no evidence, although many false witnesses appeared. Finally, two more witnesses appeared.
They said, "This man claimed, "I can destroy God's temple. I can rebuild it in three days.""
Then the high priest stood up. He asked Jesus, "Do you not want to answer? What are these charges that these men are bringing against you?"
But Jesus remained silent. Then the high priest said to him, "I adjure you by the living God, tell us if you are the Christ, the Son of God."
"Yes. As you say," Jesus answered. "But this is what I tell all of you. In the days ahead, you will see the Son of Man seated at the right hand of the Mighty One. And you will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven."
Then the high priest tore his clothes. He said, "He has spoken very evil words against God! Why do we need any more witnesses? You have heard him say these evil words.
What do you think?" "He must die!" they answered.
Then they vomited on his face. They hit him with their hands. Others clapped for him.
They said, "Tell us, O Christ! Who is it that struck you?" Peter said, "I do not know Jesus."
Peter was sitting in the courtyard. A female servant came up to him. "You also were with Jesus the Galilean," she said.
But before them all, Peter said that he was not. "I do not know what you are saying," he said.
Then he went out to the gate leading to the courtyard. There another woman saw him. She told the people, "This man was with Jesus of Nazareth."
Again he said he was not. And with an oath he said, "I do not know the man!"
Soon, those who were standing there went to Peter. "You must be one of them," they said. "The way you talk gives you away."
Then Peter began to curse himself. He swore to them, "I do not know the man!" Immediately, the cock crowed.
Then Peter remembered what Jesus had said. "A cock will not crow," Jesus had told him. "Before that happens, you will say three times that you do not know me." Peter went out. He broke down and wept.
Judah hangs himself.
It was early in the morning. All the chief priests and the elders of the people decided to put Jesus to death.
They bound him and bargained with him. Then they delivered him to Pilate, who was the governor.
Now Judas, the one who betrayed him, realized he had been condemned to die, and he went back to the leaders and the priests, who were sitting in the courtyard. He approached them and said, "I will tell you what this man has promised me. He said to me, 'If you hand him over to us, you will receive thirty pieces of silver.' And I, as I was told, handed him over to you." Then Judas, his betrayer, seeing that he was condemned to death, and overcome with remorse, went back to the leaders and the priests, and threw the pieces of silver down in the sanctuary. He said, "I have sinned by betraying innocent blood." "What is that to us?" they replied. "That is your responsibility." And Judas threw the pieces of silver down in the sanctuary and departed. And he went out and hanged himself.
He said: "I made a mistake. I handed over an innocent man." "What does it matter to us?" they replied. "This is your problem."
Judah then threw the money into the temple and left. After this, he went and hanged himself.
The leading priests chose the coins. They said, "It is against the law to put this money in the temple treasury. It is blood money. It has been paid for a man's death."
They decided to use the money to buy the pottery field. People from other countries will be buried there.
Therefore, the Field of Blood has been called by that name to this day.
Then the words spoken by the prophet Jeremiah were fulfilled. For he had said, "They took the thirty pieces of silver, the price at which the people of Israel had valued him."
They took the money and bought the potter's field, as the Lord commanded me." (Zechariah 11:12,13; Jeremiah 19:1-13; 32:6-9) They brought Jesus to Pilate
Jesus was standing before the governor. The governor asked him, "Are you the king of the Jews?" "Yes, it is as you say," Jesus answered.
But when the leading priests and the elders brought charges against him, he made no response.
Then Pilate asked him, "Don't you hear the charges they are bringing against you?"
But Jesus answered nothing to any of the charges, not to a single charge. The governor was exceedingly astonished.
It was the custom of the governor at Passover to release a single prisoner. The people could choose whom they wanted.
At that time they had a notorious prisoner named Barabbas.
When the crowd had gathered, Pilate asked them, "Which one do you want me to release? Barabbas? Or Jesus who is called the Messiah?"
Pilate knew the leaders were jealous. He knew this was the reason they were giving him Jesus.
While Pilate was sitting on the judge's chair, his wife sent him a message. It said: "Do not do anything with that man. He is not guilty. I suffered a lot in a dream today because of him."
But the chief priests and the elders incited the crowd to ask for Barabbas to be released and for Jesus to be killed.
The governor said, "Which one do you want me to release for you?" They said, "Barabbas."
"So then, what shall I do with Jesus, who is called the Messiah?" Pilate asked. They all answered, "Crucify him!"
"Why? What has he done?" Pilate asked. But they cried out all the louder, "Crucify him!"
Blassan saw that he had not come to anything. Instead of that, Friday was beginning in anger. So he took water and washed his hands in front of them. "I am not to blame for the death of this man," he said. "You are well deserving of it!"
All the people answered, "We and our children will accept the blame for his death!"
With Pilate Barabbas was released. But Jesus was flogged. Then he was handed over to be crucified on the cross. The soldiers mocked Jesus.
The governor's soldiers took Jesus to the palace, which was called the Praetorium. The rest of the soldiers gathered around him.
They stripped him of his clothes and put a purple robe on him.
Then they gathered the thorns together and made a crown. They put it on his head. They put a reed in his right hand. Then they knelt down before him and mocked him. "Hail, King of the Jews!" they said.
They spat on him. They hit him on the head with a stick again and again.
After mocking him, they stripped him of his robe. They put his clothes on him. Then they led him away to nail him to a cross. Jesus is nailed to a cross.
As they were going out of the city, they met a man from Cyrene, whose name was Simon. They compelled him to carry the cross.
They came to a place called Golgotha. The word Golgotha means the place of the skull.
There they mixed the wine with bitter spices and gave it to Jesus to drink. After tasting it, He refused to drink it.
When they crucified him, they divided his garments among themselves by casting lots.
They sat down and went to watch him there.
Above his head they placed the written charge against him. It read, ~This is Jesus, the King of the Jews.~
Two thieves were crucified with him, one on his right and the other on his left.
Those who passed by screeched at Jesus and mocked him. They shook their heads.
He said, "Are you going to destroy the temple and rebuild it in three days? Save yourself! Get down from the cross, if you are the son of God!"
In the same way the chief priests and the teachers of the law and the elders ridiculed him.
They said, "He saved others," "but he cannot save himself! He is the King of Israel! Let him come down now from the cross! Then we will believe in him."
He must trust in God. May God deliver him now if he wants him. He is the one who said: "I am the Son of God."
Likewise, the thieves who were crucified with Jesus reviled him. Jesus dies.
From noon until three o'clock, darkness covered all the earth.
About three hours, Jesus cried out loud. He said, "My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?" This means "God, God, why have you left me?" (Psalm 22:1)
Some of those who stood there heard Jesus cry out. They said, "He is calling for Elijah."
Immediately, someone ran and brought a sponge. He filled it with sour wine and put it on a reed. He offered it to Jesus to drink.
The rest said, "Leave him alone. Let's see if Elijah comes to take him out."
After Jesus cried out again in a loud voice, he died.
At that moment, the temple curtain was torn from top to bottom. The earth shook. The rocks split.
Tombs opened. The bodies of many holy people who died were brought back to life.
They came out of the tombs. After Jesus rose to life, they went to the holy city. There they appeared to many people.
