Not far away, many women watched. They had followed Jesus from Galilee to care for his needs.
Mary Magdalene was among them. Mary, the mother of James and Joseph, was also there. So was the mother of the sons of Zebedee. They buried Jesus
As evening approached, a wealthy man from the town of Arimathea came. His name was Joseph. He had become a follower of Jesus.
He went to Pilate and asked for Jesus' body. Pilate ordered that he be given it.
Joseph took the body and wrapped it in a clean linen cloth.
He placed her in his new grave which he had dug in the rock. Then he rolled a large stone in front of the entrance to the tomb. Then he went away.
Mary Magdalene and the other Mary were sitting there opposite the tomb. The guards were at the tomb.
The next day was the one after the preparation day. The chief priests and the Pharisees went to Pilate.
They said, "Sir, we remember something the deceiver said while he was still alive. He claimed, "After three days I will rise again."
So he told them to put a guard on the tomb until the third day. Otherwise, his disciples might come and take away the body. Then they would tell the people that Jesus had risen from the dead. This last lie would be worse than the first."
"Take with you some guards," Pilate answered. "Go. Make the tomb as secure as you can."
So they went and made the tomb secure. They placed a seal on the stone and posted guards on duty.
Jesus rises from the dead.
It was over on Saturday. The dawn came on the first day of the week. Mary Magdalene and the other Mary went to look at the tomb.
There was a strong earthquake. The Angel of the Lord descended from heaven. The angel went to the tomb. He rolled away the stone and sat on it.
His body seemed like lightning. His clothes are white as snow.
The guards were so afraid of him that they trembled and became like dead men.
The angel said to the women, "Do not be afraid. I know that you are looking for Jesus, who was crucified."
He was not here! He has risen, just as He said He would! Come and see the place where He lay.
Go quickly! Tell his disciples: "He has been raised from the dead. He is going ahead of you to Galilee. There you will see him." And now, I have told you."
The women hurried away from the tomb. They were afraid, but they were filled with joy. She rushed to the disciples to tell them.
Suddenly Jesus met them. "Greetings!" He said. They came to Him, held His feet and worshipped Him.
Then Jesus said to them, "Do not be afraid. Go and tell my brothers to go to Galilee. There they will see me." The guards went and informed the chief priests.
And while the women were on their way, some of the guards went to the city. And they told the chief priests everything that had happened.
When the chief priests and elders met, they devised a plan. They gave a large sum of money to the soldiers.
They told the soldiers: "We want you to say, 'His followers came by night. They stole his body while we slept.'"
If the Governor hears this report, we'll pay him off. This will keep you out of trouble."
The soldiers took the money and did as they were told. This story spread among the Jews to this day. The final instructions of Jesus to his disciples.
Then the twelve disciples went to Galilee. They went to the mountain that Jesus had told them to go to.
And when they saw him, they prostrated themselves to him, but some of them continued in doubt.
Then Jesus came to them and said, "All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me.
Go therefore and make disciples of all nations. Baptize them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit.
Teach them to obey all that I have commanded you. And you can be sure that I am always with you, to the end."
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
This is the beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God.
The prophet Isaiah long ago wrote, "I send My messenger to prepare the way before Me." (Malachi 3:1)
A messenger calls in the desert, 'Prepare the way for the Lord. Make his paths straight.' ' (Isaiah 40:3)
And so John came. He was baptizing people in the desert. He also preached that people must be baptized and repent of their sins. Then God would forgive them.
All the country people of Judea streamed out to him, and the people of Jerusalem as well. When they confessed their sins, John baptized them in the river Jordan.
John wore clothes made of camel hair. He had a leather belt around his waist. He ate locusts and wild honey.
This is how John was preaching: "After me, someone will come who is stronger than me. I am not good enough to lean down and untie his sandals."
I baptize you with water. But he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit." Jesus is baptized and tempted.
At that time Jesus came from Nazareth in Galilee. John baptized him in the Jordan River.
Jesus was coming up out of the water. Suddenly, he saw the heavens opened up. He saw the Holy Spirit descend on him like a dove.
A voice spoke to him from heaven, saying, "You are my Son, whom I love; with pleasure I am well-pleased with you."
Immediately the Spirit sent Jesus out into the desert.
He was in the desert for 40 days. There the devil tempted him. Wild animals did not harm Jesus. Angels watched over him. Jesus chooses the first disciples.
After John was put in prison, Jesus went into Galilee, proclaiming the good news of God.
He said, "The time has come," "The Kingdom of God is near. Repent of your sins and believe in the good news!"
Once Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee. There he saw Simon and his brother Andrew. They were casting fishing nets into the lake. They were fishermen.
Jesus said, "Come, follow Me, and I will make you fishers of men."
Immediately they left their nets and followed him.
Then Jesus walked a little farther. And while he was doing this, he saw James, the son of Zebedee, and his brother John. They were in a boat preparing their nets.
Immediately he called them. They left their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired men. Then they followed Jesus. Jesus Expels an Evil Spirit
Jesus and his disciples came to Capernaum. When the Sabbath came, he went to the synagogue. There he began to teach.
People were amazed at his teaching, for he taught them as one who had authority, not like the teachers of the law.
Suddenly, a man in their temple screamed. He was under the control of an evil spirit. He said:
"What do you want from us, Jesus of Nazareth? Have you come to destroy us? I know who you are. You are the holy one of God!"
Jesus said firmly, "Be quiet!" "Come out of him!"
The evil spirit violently shook the man. Then it came out of him with a loud scream.
Everyone was astonished. They asked one another, "What is this? A new teaching! And with such authority! He even gives orders to evil spirits, and they obey him."
News about Jesus spread quickly throughout Galilee. Jesus heals many people.
Jesus and those with him left the synagogue. Immediately they went with James and John to the house of Simon and Andrew.
Simon's mother-in-law was lying in bed. She was suffering from a fever. They told Jesus about her.
He went to her, took her hand and helped her up. The fever left her. Then she began to serve them.
That evening, after sunset, people brought to Jesus all who were sick. They also brought all who were possessed by demons.
All the people in the town gathered at the door.
Jesus healed many of them. They were suffering from all kinds of diseases. He also drove demons out of many. But He did not allow the demons to speak, because they knew who He was. Jesus prayed in a quiet place.
It was very early and the darkness was still present. Jesus got up and left the house. He went to a place where he could be alone. There he prayed.
Simon and his friends went to look for Jesus.
When they found him, they shouted, "Everyone has been looking for you!"
Jesus answered, "Let us go to some other place. I want to go to the nearby towns. I must preach there also. That is why I came."
He traveled throughout the Galilee region, teaching in their synagogues and also driving out demons. Jesus taught a man who was suffering from a severe skin disease.
A man who was suffering from a skin disease came to Jesus. On his knees, he asked Jesus. He said, "If you are willing to make me 'clean', you can do so."
Jesus was filled with deep concern. He reached out and touched the man. "I am willing to do it," he said. "Be clean!"
Immediately it left him, the illness. He was cured.
Jesus made the man go immediately. He gave the man a strong warning.
He said, "Don't tell anyone about this," "Go and make yourselves appear before the priest. Make the sacrifices that Moses commanded. He will be a witness before the priest and the people that you are 'clean'." "
But the man went out and began to talk directly. He spread the news to everyone. So Jesus could no longer enter the city openly. He stayed outside in secluded places. But people still came to him from everywhere.
Jesus heals a man who could not walk.
A few days later, Jesus entered Capernaum again. The people heard that he had returned to his house.
Many people gathered until there was no more room. There was no more room even outside the door. And Jesus spoke the word to them.
Four of those who came were carrying a man who could not walk.
But they could not bring him near to Jesus because of the crowd. So they went up to the roof of the house where Jesus was and opened a hole in the roof. Then they lowered the man on his mat through the hole in the roof.
When Jesus saw their faith, he said to the man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
Some teachers of the law were sitting there. They were thinking.
Why is this man speaking like this? He is saying something very evil! Only God can forgive sins!
Jesus immediately understood what they were thinking in their hearts. So he said to them, "Why are you thinking these things in your hearts?"
Is it easier to say to this man, "Your sins are forgiven"? or to say, "Rise, take up your bed and walk"?
"I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." Then Jesus spoke to the man who could not walk.
He said, "I'm telling you, get up. Take your rug and go back to your house."
The man stood up and took his mat. Then he walked away while everyone was watching. Everyone was amazed. They praised God and said, "We have never seen anything like this!" Jesus Chooses Lazarus
Again Jesus went out beside the Sea of Galilee. A great crowd came to him. He began to teach them.
And because he was walking, he saw Louie'ei son of Alfaa'i. And Louie'ei was sitting on the place of the collection officer of the offices. So Jesus said to him, "Follow me." So Louie'ei arose and followed him.
After this, Jesus was having dinner at the house of Lazarus. Many tax collectors and "sinners" were eating with him and his disciples. They were part of the large crowd that was following Jesus.
There were some teachers of the law who were Pharisees. They saw Jesus eating with "sinners" and tax collectors. So they asked his disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
Jesus heard that. He said to them, "Those who are healthy do not need a physician; the sick do. I did not come for those who believe that they are righteous before God to come to me. I came for sinners to follow me." Jesus is asked about fasting.
The disciples of John and of the Pharisees would fast. The disciples of John came to Jesus. They said to Him, "Why do the disciples of John fast, and the disciples of the Pharisees also, but Your disciples do not fast?" Jesus said to them, "Can the wedding guests fast while the bridegroom is with them? But the days will come when the bridegroom is taken away from them, and then they will fast in those days. "No one sews a patch of unshrunk cloth on an old garment. Otherwise, the new patch will pull away from the old garment, and the tear will be worse. "Also, no one pours new wine into old wineskins. Otherwise, the new wine will burst the skins, spill the wine, and ruin the skins. But new wine must be poured into new wineskins, and both are preserved."
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom fast while he is with them? They cannot fast as long as he is with them."
But the time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them. On that day they will go away without eating.
People do not put a new piece of cloth on old clothes. If they did, the new piece would tear away from the old. This would make the hole worse.
People don't pour new wine into old wineskins. If they do, the wine will burst the wineskins. Then the wine is ruined and the wineskins are destroyed. No, people pour new wine into new wineskins." The Lord is Lord of the Sabbath.
One Sabbath, Jesus was walking through grainfields with his disciples. As they walked, the disciples began to pluck heads of grain and rub them in their hands.
The Pharisees said to Jesus, "Look, if someone does this on the Sabbath, it is against the law. Why do your disciples do this?"
He said, "Have you read what David did? He and his companions were hungry. They needed food."
That was when Abiathar was high priest. David went into the house of God and ate the holy bread. Only the priests were allowed to eat it. David also gave some of it to his men."
Then Jesus said to them, "The Sabbath was made for man, not man for the Sabbath.
The master of the human is the master of the Sabbath day as well."
Saturday recovery
Last time Jesus went to the synagogue. There was a man with a weak and deformed hand.
Some of the Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. They were watching him closely. They wanted to see if he would heal the man on the Sabbath.
Jesus said to the man with the withered and deformed hand, "Stand up in front of everyone."
Then Jesus asked them, "What does the Law say we must do on the Sabbath? Must we do good? Or must we do evil? Must we save life? Or must we kill?" But no one answered him.
Jesus looked around at them angrily. He was so upset because their hearts were so stubborn. Then He said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." He stretched it out, and his hand was as good as new.
Then the Pharisees went out and began to plot with the Herodians against Jesus, seeking to kill him. The people gathered behind Jesus.
Jesus went with his disciples to the Sea of Galilee. A great crowd from Galilee followed him.
People heard about all the things Jesus was doing. Many people came to him. They came from Judea, Jerusalem, and Edom. They came from the territories east of the Jordan River. And they came from the area around Tyre and Sidon.
Because of the crowd, Jesus asked his disciples to prepare a small boat for him. This would prevent people from crowding him.
Jesus had healed many, so the sick would crawl forward to touch him.
When the people saw those possessed by demons, they fell prostrate before him. The demons cried out, "You are the Son of God!"
But Jesus commanded them not to tell who he was. Jesus appoints the twelve apostles.
Jesus went up a mountain. He called some people to come to him, and they came.
From among them he chose 12 and named them apostles. And from that time they were with him. And he also sent them to preach.
They have authority over demons.
Jesus appointed the twelve. Simon was one of them. Jesus named him Peter.
There was James the son of Zebedee, and his brother John. Jesus gave them the name Boanerges. Boanerges means Sons of Thunder.
There was also Andrew, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew, Thomas, and James the son of Alphaeus. And there was Thaddaeus and Simon the Zealot.
Gades Esau was one of them too. He was the one who would later deliver Jesus into the hands of his enemies. Jesus and Beelzebub
Jesus entered a house. A crowd gathered again. It was so large that Jesus and his disciples couldn't even eat.
The man's family heard about this. So they went to take care of him. They said, "He's crazy."
There were some teachers of the law there. They had come all the way from Jerusalem. "He is controlled by Beelzebul!" they exclaimed. "By the prince of demons he is driving out demons!"
Jesus called them and spoke to them in parables. He said, "How can a demon cast out a demon?"
If a kingdom fights against itself, it cannot stand.
If a family is divided, it cannot stand.
